:: R6-NPC-JessMorning [release6]
<<scene "img/scenes/jess-working-graphics.png" "MarcKing LIVE, Charity Stream Marathon, 48 Hours">>

A rare morning where Jess is actually here. She's at the kitchen table with coffee and her laptop, working on something. I can see the screen from the counter where I'm pouring cereal. She's got a design tool open. A graphic. Marcus's face, professionally lit, with text overlay, "MarcKing LIVE, Charity Stream Marathon, 48 Hours."

//"Forty eight hour charity stream?"// I say.

She looks up. //"Yeah. It's going to be huge. We're partnering with three nonprofits and a gaming chair company is matching donations up to fifty K."// Her eyes are bright. That brightness. The one I remember from when she used to plan my milestone streams. //"I've been designing all the overlay graphics, the countdown timer, the donation tracker display."//

She turns the laptop toward me. The graphics are gorgeous. Clean, professional, Marcus's brand colors. She put real work into these. Real talent. The kind of focused creative energy that used to go into my channel.

//"These look amazing, Jess."//

//"Thanks."// She smiles. Real smile. Then her phone buzzes. She glances at it. The smile changes. Softens into something private. She texts back with one thumb, fast, and puts the phone face down on the table.

//"So,"// she says, turning back to me. //"I saw the sponsor email in your inbox. The wig company?"//

I freeze. //"You saw that?"//

//"Your laptop was open on the counter this morning. I wasn't snooping, it was just there. LunaLocks?"//

//"Yeah. They want to sponsor EthX-chan streams."//

//"Ethan, that's amazing."// She leans forward. Genuinely excited. //"Two hundred per stream? That's not nothing, and with the donation numbers from the other night."//

She does the math out loud. Same math I already did. Same numbers. Same conclusion. But hearing her say it makes it different. More real. More like a plan and less like a surrender.

//"You should do it,"// she says.

//"You think?"//

//"Babe, your regular streams are flatlined. I love you and I believe in your talent, but the numbers don't lie. This is a door. Walk through it."//

The advice is good. The advice is correct. The advice comes from the same woman who walked through her own door six weeks ago when Marcus DM'd her, and hasn't fully come back through mine since.

//"Your cereal's getting soggy,"// she says, nodding at my bowl.

I eat soggy cereal and think about doors.

